The finished project is in the zip. It uses PokéAPI because it needs no key, has no meaningful rate limit, and has been reliable for years. It's built on your template, and the README and deploy workflow are unchanged. It compiles cleanly, and I tested clicking from the list to a detail page and then Next in a headless browser.

What's in it, mapped to the rubric

List view (/): the search filters as you type by name or number. You can sort by number, name, height, weight or base experience, with ascending and descending buttons.
Gallery (/gallery): a grid of official artwork with type filters. You can select several types and choose whether a Pokémon must have any or all of them.
Detail view (/pokemon/:id): shows artwork, types, height, weight, base XP, abilities and stat bars.
Previous and Next step through whatever list you clicked from, such as only the "char" search results or only Fire types. They wrap around at the ends, and the left and right arrow keys work too.
Opening a URL directly falls back to stepping through all 151.
Required tools: TypeScript, React Router, Axios and CSS Modules, with no inline styles or tables. normalize.css is included.
Deep links: npm run build copies index.html to 404.html. Without this, opening /mp2/pokemon/25 directly on GitHub Pages would show a GitHub 404 page.
Other details:
Data is cached in sessionStorage, so the API is only called once per visit.
If loading fails, a message explains why and offers a Try again button.
Search and filter settings are saved in the URL, so going Back keeps them.
