dude git add added everything including node module4s

That almost always means the hidden .gitignore didn't get copied. Finder and most file explorers skip dotfiles when you drag files over. Here's the fix.

1. Recreate .gitignore by running this in your mp2 folder:

bash
printf "node_modules\ndist\n*.local\n.DS_Store\n" > .gitignore
On Windows PowerShell, use this instead:

powershell
"node_modules`ndist`n*.local`n.DS_Store" | Out-File -Encoding utf8 .gitignore
2. Untrack node_modules. This doesn't delete the folder; it only stops git from tracking it.

bash
git rm -r --cached node_modules dist --ignore-unmatch -q
git add .
git status
git status should now list only your source files, package.json, package-lock.json, .gitignore and the other config files. It should not show anything under node_modules.

3. Commit and push.

bash
git commit -m "Pokédex MP2"
git branch -M main
git push origin main
If you had already committed with node_modules but haven't pushed yet, the commit in step 3 removes it from the latest version, but the old commit still contains it. To get a clean history, run this before step 3:

bash
git reset --soft HEAD~1
Then do steps 2 and 3 again. If you already pushed, steps 1 to 3 are fine as they are. The deploy runs npm ci from scratch either way, so a stray old commit won't break anything.

Also check that .github/workflows/deploy.yml made it into the repo. It's hidden for the same reason, and without it nothing will deploy. Run ls -a .github/workflows to confirm. If it's missing, copy it over from the zip's mp2-main/.github folder.

